WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.888
Imagine sitting inside a device.

00:00:03.013 --> 00:00:05.221
You can see what changes around you.

00:00:05.346 --> 00:00:08.658
Can you work out which controls someone
operated outside?

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:11.810
The model sees images from inside.

00:00:11.935 --> 00:00:16.183
The outside view from the original video lets
us follow along.

00:00:16.308 --> 00:00:23.844
The split has 9,000 training episodes, 1,000
validation episodes, and 200 test episodes.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.002
Watch the cabin between frames five and six.

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:31.295
The final event was braking while turning left.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.060
The model said braking while turning right.

00:00:34.185 --> 00:00:37.929
That direction error costs the whole sequence
its point.

00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:46.824
Across 20 devices in five domains of physics,
the test asks 1,180 questions, spanning one to

00:00:46.824 --> 00:00:47.725
six steps.

00:00:48.325 --> 00:00:56.989
In Figure 1, GPT-6.1 Sol gets 136 of the 200
six-step questions right.

00:00:57.114 --> 00:00:59.034
That’s 68 percent.

00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:08.182
Qwen3.5-9B scores half a percent at baseline,
20 percent after supervised fine-tuning, and

00:01:08.182 --> 00:01:13.417
31.8 percent with SimPO added, averaged over
two seeds.

